# Two parts of one composed layer, drawn from one filing, with what each contributes. One row per
# ordered pair of part layers that the same fusion absorbs from the same part filing, carrying each
# side's conversion factor.
#
# The self-join inside one fusion
#   This is the self-join inside one fusion, and it is not the other one. Two self-joins of
#   composition/parts.sqlc live in this tree and they answer opposite questions:
#     here                       join on the composed layer -> two parts inside one total
#     eliminations/derived.sqlc  join on the part           -> one part inside two totals
#   The first is a fusion's internal structure, which is what an `asrt:Elimination` speaks about:
#   its `between` names which of its own parts overlap. The second is two documents reaching for
#   the same supply, which is a referral to a person. Same operands, different key, and unifying
#   them would be a category error.
#
# Ordered, because its caller needs the order
#   It is ordered rather than unordered. A coupling has a direction, `from` and `to`, so
#   reports/coupling_absorbed_by_a_fusion.sqlc must be able to look up the pair the coupling names
#   in the order the coupling names it. Its sibling eliminations/derived.sqlc takes the row
#   comparison `<` instead, because an overlap has no direction and reporting it twice would be
#   reporting it twice.
#
# The reflexive pair is kept on purpose
#   A layer is trivially a sibling of itself, and the caller wants that row: a coupling whose ends
#   are the same layer is unreachable through pm.coupling's own key, so excluding it here would be
#   guarding against something the schema already forbids while silently changing what a future
#   caller sees.
-- composition/parts.sqlc crossed with itself on the composed layer and the part filing.
SELECT x.composition, x.composed_layer,
       x.part_filing,
       x.part_layer AS from_layer,
       y.part_layer AS to_layer,
       x.factor_low  AS from_factor_low,  x.factor_mode AS from_factor_mode,
       y.factor_low  AS to_factor_low,    y.factor_mode AS to_factor_mode
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) x
JOIN (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) y
  ON y.composition    = x.composition
 AND y.composed_layer = x.composed_layer
 AND y.part_filing    = x.part_filing
